          "type": "concept",
          "title": "George Wythe the Mentor",
          "content": "After graduation Jefferson studied law for five years under George Wythe, the most learned lawyer in Virginia. Wythe later signed the Declaration alongside his pupil, became America's first law professor, and trained John Marshall and Henry Clay. Jefferson called Wythe his second father, and the long apprenticeship grounded his radical politics in deep classical learning.",
